    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 180022\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n522 PM PDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dry conditions through the weekend with near normal temperatures\n\n* Chances of precipitation return early next week, then a warming \n  trend expected late next week \n\n &&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Wednesday.\n\nDrier conditions are setting up for the rest of the week and into \nthe weekend for the Desert Southwest as an upper trough over \nNorthern California pushes inland. Observations show widespread \nmonsoonal moisture still lingering in the Mojave Desert and through \nArizona as dew points are still in the 50s and 60s for many areas. \nLatest ACARs and 18z Las Vegas sounding both confirm a warm nose \naround 700mb which is essentially capping the environment, \npreventing convective development for the rest of the day. A stray \nshower or two is possible in far Eastern Lincoln or Mohave County; \nhowever nothing impactful is likely to occur. Downsloping westerly \nflow off the Sierra will help dry the atmosphere out over the next \n24 to 36 hours. Drier air quickly advects eastward tomorrow into \nSaturday as the aforementioned trough pushes inland, significantly \ndropping dew points and low level moisture locally and keeping \nprecipitation chances near zero into the weekend. While guidance \ndoes show below normal 850mb temperatures associated with this \ntrough, downsloping from higher terrain should offset that and keep \nsurface temperatures near normal for this time of year. Expect max \ntemperatures in desert valleys to range from the 90s to low 100s \nwith cooler temperatures in higher elevations.\n\nBrief ridging builds overhead for the weekend; however another upper \ntrough is right on its heals as it approaches Southern and Central \nCalifornia late Sunday. Upper level moisture advection ahead of the \ntrough should bring widespread high clouds to the region beginning \nSunday, then low level moisture increases Monday into Tuesday as the \nmid and upper low push inland into Central Nevada. Ensemble means \nindicate a resurgence of PWATs in excess of 1.00 inch early next \nweek for the Colorado River Valley and Southern Nevada. Cooler air \naloft under the trough will help increase instability necessary to \nbring a return of PoPs to the region, with best chances across the \nMojave Desert Monday and Tuesday. At the moment, it looks like \nprecipitation will be in the form of showers and a few storms, \nlikely hit or miss with breaks in between. Current QPF looks fairly \nlow and some spots could remain dry through the period. With the \npresence of clouds and precipitation, expect near normal \ntemperatures the beginning of next week.\n\nBy Mid-week, mid and long range guidance are coming into consensus \nthat ridging returns to the Desert Southwest, which will allow \ntemperatures to climb back to above-normal for this time of year. \nThe chances of at least Moderate Heat Risk are at or above 50% \nWednesday through the end of next week, particularly for desert \nvalleys and the CRV. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...\nSoutherly winds with gusts of 20 to 25 knots are expected into the \nearly evening before speeds diminish to less than 10 knots after \nsunset while remaining out of the south to southwest. Similar winds \nare expected on Friday with gusts to around 35 knots possible in the \nafternoon, although directions are expected to be more southerly \nthan what was seen this afternoon. VFR conditions will prevail \nthrough Friday with FEW to SCT clouds with bases remaining 10kft AGL \nor higher. \n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Gusty south to southwest \nwinds are expected across much of the region into early this \nevening, with occasional gusts of 20 to 25 knots likely in many \nareas. Slightly higher gusts to around 30 knots are possible in the \nnorthern Owens Valley, where up-valley southerly flow will continue. \nMost locations will see winds diminish after sunset, although winds \nacross the western Mojave Desert will remain elevated into the late \nevening before gradually decreasing. FEW to SCT clouds with bases \nremaining AOA 10kft AGL are expected into the evening, with mainly \nclear skies developing thereafter and continuing through Friday. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
